A||||18||88formula||How do 88 fiero's do in SCCA events?||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||04-27-2001||08:25 PM||||I was wondering how well 88 fiero's do in SCCA events and what class they would be in? I have a 5-speed v6 formula with a high performance 3.4-liter engine. What kind of cars do they compare to for times? They won’t know it is a 3.4-liter engine though. They will see the FOCOA headers and Borla exhaust though. <P>My suspension modifications include Polly bushings everywhere, KYB shocks and Struts, and stock size Cooper Cobra radial CTH tires. The tires came with the car. Everything else is stock.<P>I was thinking of trying out an autocross in my area but I have never been to one before. <P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||04-27-2001||08:59 PM||||Depends on the driver...Competition MR2,VW Passant(?) Just go for it.. The guy I bought my 88 from had used it in SCCA events, he said it ran real good.  Thats probably why I've had to replace the engine and suspension.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Greg Piet||04-27-2001||09:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR><B> They won’t know it is a 3.4-liter engine though. They will see the FOCOA headers and Borla exhaust though. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I sincerely hope you are not contemplating the idea of entering a class in which your car would be illegal.  <BR>I have raced my Fiero in SCCA events and been in my correct class and always in the top of the pack...  There is nothing more frustrating than getting beaten by someone who does not follow the rules.  <P>ANY engine mod puts you into the Modified bracket.  You would be in EM agianst the big boys and get your ass spanked by vehicles with 3 times the horsepower and racing slicks.  To be blunt I can beat most v8's excluding Z06's that run street tires and stock suspension while I am on my DOT legal race tires and a moderately aggressive suspension package and a 2.8 v6.  I didnt see an Eibach kit, coil overs, or swaybars listed so you would likely lose to any car that uses near the maximum allowed in Street Prepared<P>There is another class called SM which is street modified...  It only allows 4 seat vehicles that are street legal, but many regions are adopting the SM2 class which allows 2 seaters, or modding the SM rules to allow 2 seaters in the SM class.<P><P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72 Race Director<BR>#47 CSP<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.physics.unlv.edu/~gregpiet/Jan1617s.jpg">   <BR>Our lady of blessed acceleration dont fail me now!||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||04-27-2001||10:43 PM||||What fun is it to race against a car with three times the horsepower and slicks?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||LZeitgeist||04-27-2001||11:56 PM||||What fun is it to race against a car with an illegally-classed 3.4-litre engine?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||04-28-2001||12:24 AM||||What are they going to do, make you tear your engine down so they can measure it?  Don't sweat it. You don't want to race against guys in emod on slicks.  Go to the tech and let them decide what class you should be in.  And don't tell them anything.  No one cares till you start winning stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||04-28-2001||12:32 AM||||I don't think its fare to be put in a class with cars that have 600 horsepower, racing suspensions, and tires. I just want to have a little fun and try it out. They will put me in a class where I'm outclassed.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Greg Piet||04-28-2001||01:07 AM||||If you are really just in it for fun, then your cars class doesnt matter now does it??? YOU could put yourself into F125 and it shouldnt matter(since it is your responsibility to properly class your car in Solo2)...<BR>Just compare your times to other cars you think you should beat.<BR>If the above is true, read no more...  If the above is not true please consider the following...<BR>Please dont screw over people in that class that might actually be trying to compete (Some people actually get sponsorships depending on their performance even in the stock classes).<P>The actual rules go as follows<BR>First it is YOUR responsibility to properly class your vehicle.<BR>Second if someone lodges a formal complaint the following happens... The owner of the vehicle pays to have the item of question removed and then examined by an official.  If the abovementioned item is deemed to be illegal then the owner of the vehicle pays to have the car put back together... if the part is deemed to be legal then the challenger pays to have the car put back together.<P>And my favorite...  once someone gets caught cheating as blatently as what I am reading here... good luck finding any experienced racer who will give you the time of day. <P><BR>And as a personal note, I get alot of compliments from people surprised about how well my Fiero does.  Please dont give the Fiero the rep in other regions as a cheaters car, and dont put the little idea in my head that I need to go over other Fieros I compete with with a fine toothed comb.  The rules are there for a reason! <P>Yes in a local event you will not be questioned, maybe not even in a divisional.  I dont know about anyone else on this forum, but a victory while cheating is no victory at all. There are always rules that are silly... for example all bushings to be in stock class should be OEM, and not just "meets OEM standards".  This rule deviation is a FAR FAR less noticable deviation than an increase in engine displacement of over 20%.  Whats to say then that some other guy comes down to the track with his v8 Fiero pushing out only 300 hp should race with the 600hp fieros???  well????  We will just put that wimpy v8 Fiero against the Iron Duke Fiero.||||reg||8
Z||000008||88formula||04-28-2001||10:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Greg Piet:<BR><B>If you are really just in it for fun, then your cars class doesnt matter now does it??? YOU could put yourself into F125 and it shouldnt matter(since it is your responsibility to properly class your car in Solo2)...<BR>Just compare your times to other cars you think you should beat.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Your right and you've convinced me. I guess what I need to do is get the rules book and go over the rules. <P>What tire pressures do you run your car through the course with? What modifications did you do to your fiero suspension and how well do you do in the solo II event against other cars in your class? What cars do you compete against?<P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Wannabe||04-28-2001||11:27 AM||||In some series, the '88 Fiero can actually be a bit of a sleeper in that it is classified the same as the earlier cars. For tight, low speed events, there is not too much of a difference, but for running on a race course, where you need confidence in the car to be able to go flat out into high speed corners, there is a huge difference. The biggest drawback of the '88 Fiero in that case becomes lack of power. BTW, the 3.4 block can be easily identified by the strengthing ribs along the outside walls  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Black88GT||04-28-2001||03:11 PM||||I'm sure the guys at the local SCCA event are going to notice the ribs and figure its a 3.4 swap...lol<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Slammed Fiero||04-28-2001||03:24 PM||||3.4ltr engine or not , it won't matter if it's your first autocross there are far more important things than how much Hp you have.  Civics and other smaller more nimble cars with half the Hp with good drivers will likley better your times. Remember good driver on the street doesn't mean good driver at an autocross. Just be smooth . squeeze the throttle don't stab it. It will take a long time before they care about what motor you have in your car<P><BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86GT<BR>Shown , autocrossed , abused...||||reg||1
Z||000012||Tom88gt||04-29-2001||09:34 PM||||I raced SCCA a couple of times.  I was placed in CS class against late 90's MR2s and Miatas.  I was way out of the ball part in power to weight.  The SCCA tech guys though our Fieros had about 150-170 horses and were shocked to discover how much the car really weighs.  <P>Needless to say, I was flattered to find that the SCCA thinks my mid 80's GM is equal to late 90's Imports.<P>But also disgusted at the unfairness (in my eyes) of the class I was in.<P>I personally think a 3.4 will put your power/weight on even ground in CS class.<P>I just don't think there are enough Fieros in SCCA for them to really care about classing the Fiero fairly.  FOr what it's worth...I saw a stock 1975 914 and 1972 911 get placed in AS class against brand new "Super-cars".<P>Finally...YES, I did come in last when I raced.  But I'm not blaming THAT on the SCCA rules.  I'm just new at it.   ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Steve Normington||04-30-2001||12:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR>Depends on the driver...<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ditto on that. I've seen a bone stock Geo Metro better a Vette's time on the slalom course at my local track.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||thomas_l||04-30-2001||12:55 PM||||Sounds like you would be in E mod - just make sure you have lots of fun racing. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007130.html" TARGET=_blank>This thread</A> has a link to my video from yesterday. There was one other Fiero there that I had seen before (they actually got me to thinking on a Fiero for SOLO-II racing) and finally met the owners. Theirs is a 88 Formula with aftermarket springs & shocks and a header. I am waiting to see the times get posted to see how they did versus my pretty much bone stock 87 GT. I am running novice and plan to do so on street tires for the rest of this year to learn to drive the Fiero. This winter I'll get another set of rims and buy racing rubber and start shooting for points in C-stock. For now I am running in Novice class which is for newbies driving whatever. For now it ain't fair but I'm not after trophies so who cares. Next year I could technically get kicked into prepared or modified because the junkyard transimission I got ended up being a 4 speed instead of 5 (no 4 speeds on 87's), but I'll cross that protest bridge when I get to it. It's not like I deliberately intended to cheat. Since I am not restoring it and only using the first two gears anyway, I decided to keep it as is. The more I see of the competition in classes, the more I think I'll leave my car stock. I too have seen Z06's out there either being under or over driven get whipped by someone in a rusty old 70's Corolla or Wabbit. You can't win em all  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000015||ChadMan||04-30-2001||01:13 PM||||To cheat or not to cheat...this is an interesting debate.  On the one hand, cheating is bad, period.<P>On the other hand, the Solo II classification rules DEFINITELY seem stacked against the Fiero, especially the 4-banger which runs in E stock.  Most of the class-leading E Stock cars weigh less and have significantly more HP than the 4-cyl Fiero:  SOHC Neons, non-VTEC Integras, the Escort GT's with the 16v Mazda motor, 16v Sciroccos, 16v Saturns, etc.  The rules are stacked against the V6 Fiero in C Stock too, but not as severely.  <P>This seems like anti-Fiero snobbery to me...after all, most SCCA guys are into foreign machinery.<P>Will I cheat?  Almost certainly not...there's no money at stake here, after all.  But I can certainly understand why someone would -- especially if he's been taking a lot of crap from some mouthy bastard in a Miata.||||reg||5
Z||000016||DRH||04-30-2001||01:42 PM||||Another way to think of it is that maybe part of the reason Fieros are classed the way they are is because people cheated in the past. Car classing is based partially on past performance of vehicles.<P>Fieros are probably being moved down next year. Part of the competition is going with it, but it should help get some of the faster cars out of the class.<P>As friendly and helpful as EVERYONE has been at the few events I've been to, there is NO WAY I could lie about what's in my car... I'd rather lose honestly than win by cheating. <P>Also remember... The MR2 and Miata guys may not know it's a 3.4, but if you happened to run into another Fiero, they'll know somethings up after your first run. <P>&lt;stepping down from soap box&gt;<P>Back to the original question... Keeping the same number of cylinders and motor manufacturer MAY put you in Prepared instead of Modified... Check the rules.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||88formula||04-30-2001||05:27 PM||||mmmmmmmmmmmm Interesting.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Eric||04-30-2001||06:25 PM||||I race to have fun.  I race a modified 88 GT.  It has a 3.1 crank and pistons and a bigger cam.  All things considered, it has like 160-170 horsepower.  I don't want to cheat, but what I'm not telling the tech people doesn't make me feel guilty at all.  I'll put it in a street prepped class this year because of some suspension mods I made. Which, I believe, give me a measurable advantage over a stock Fiero unlike the modded engine. ||||reg||1
